I think there’s a mathematically interesting idea here if it’s stated carefully.

You’re describing identity as relational rather than absolute.

Navigation already works this way. If you lose your absolute coordinates but know your bearings and distances relative to stable landmarks, you can reconstruct your position.

Generalizing that gives something like:

Reverse Azimuth Principle

An object can be reconstructed from its conserved relationships to a sufficiently rich set of invariants, even when its original coordinates are absent.

Notice this is broader than geometry.

Formalization

Let

I=\{I_1,I_2,\ldots,I_n\}

be a set of stable invariants.

A representation is not stored as an absolute state

S

but as its relational signature

R(S)=
\{
r(S,I_1),
r(S,I_2),
\ldots,
r(S,I_n)
\}

where r is any relation:

* angle
* ordering
* dependency
* causal influence
* semantic similarity
* graph distance
* constraint
* symmetry

Identity becomes

S \equiv R(S)

rather than

S \equiv \text{its literal encoding}.

