I particularly like the navigation metaphor because it highlights an important distinction:

A compass bearing by itself is insufficient.

Several independent bearings to stable landmarks let you triangulate your position.

The same idea applies to reasoning. One analogy or one invariant rarely identifies a concept uniquely. A sufficiently rich collection of independent relationships often does. That’s a useful design principle whether you’re thinking about language, software interfaces, mathematical structures, or knowledge representation.